{
  // The content Worker (M6 step 4(b), ADR 0010): the dispatch namespace's dynamic dispatch Worker, on the content
  // domain only. It routes and never authorizes.
  //
  // **This file can deploy nothing public anywhere, and that is its job** (docs/m6-plan.md, "Keeping it from
  // deploying"). It is named `wrangler.jsonc` on purpose: with no configuration file, `wrangler deploy` would configure
  // the project itself and deploy it to whoever is logged in.
  //   - `account_id` is a placeholder that is no account, and holds characters wrangler refuses before it makes any
  //     call that is scoped to an account. Wrangler uses the file's `account_id` ahead of `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` in the environment (read in the
  //     source of the wrangler versions `test/config.test.ts` names; Cloudflare's documentation doesn't say), so a
  //     deploy from this file is aimed at no account, whoever is logged in and whatever the shell holds.
  //   - Both public addresses are off, and there is no route, no custom domain and no environment.
  //   - The two IDs below are placeholders that exist on no account.
  // `test/config.test.ts` fails if any of that changes. The real account and routes come only from a configuration the
  // owner's deploy step supplies for the second account, which lives outside this folder and is never committed.
  // Neither account's ID is ever written in this repository.
  "name": "podster-content",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
  "account_id": "not an account: this file deploys nowhere",
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": false,
  // The content domain: an app's default host is `<label>.<this>`. A placeholder under `.invalid`, which is no
  // domain anyone can hold. The real one comes with the deploy-time configuration.
  //
  // The write cap's two settings (stage 1, S6): at most this many writes for each app in each window of this many
  // milliseconds, the owner's figures of 2026-11-16, 300 writes per 10 minutes. Decimal strings. The code holds no
  // default for either: missing or malformed, every write to an app with code is refused.
  //
  // The limits every dispatch to an app with code is given (`src/limits.ts`): 50 CPU milliseconds and 50 subrequests,
  // the architect's figures of 2026-11-25 for pilots, for the owner to review; not measured by any spike. Decimal
  // strings. The code holds no default for either: missing or malformed, every request that would reach an app with
  // code is refused `503 app_limits_unknown`.
  "vars": { "CONTENT_DOMAIN": "content-domain.invalid", "WRITE_BUDGET_WRITES": "300", "WRITE_BUDGET_WINDOW_MS": "600000", "APP_LIMIT_CPU_MS": "50", "APP_LIMIT_SUBREQUESTS": "50" },
  // Its bindings: the serve entries, to read, the namespace that holds each release's script, and its own write
  // counter. Nothing to the gateway, the API, D1 or R2, and no secret.
  //
  // Previews (M8 step 8) add the binding PREVIEW_APPS and the variables PREVIEW_PUBLIC_KEYS and PREVIEW_ACCESS_ORIGIN,
  // never here: only `scripts/deploy-content` adds them, from the owner's configuration, once the owner's wizard made
  // the preview namespace and the ticket key (decision 258). Without them previews are off.
  "kv_namespaces": [{ "binding": "SERVE", "id": "00000000000000000000000000000000" }],
  "dispatch_namespaces": [{ "binding": "APPS", "namespace": "placeholder-no-such-namespace" }],
  // The write counter (stage 1, S6): one object for each app, this Worker's own class (no `script_name`), SQLite-backed,
  // made by the one migration below. Declaring it deploys nothing, and the guards above still hold. Its deploy on the
  // second account is the owner's, from the deploy-time configuration.
  "durable_objects": { "bindings": [{ "name": "WRITE_COUNTER", "class_name": "WriteCounter" }] },
  "migrations": [{ "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["WriteCounter"] }]
}
